#{[17592186110810 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1045 "Linear Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186111678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186110810 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f14be6fb-7490-5e50-9e2d-7ccd72daa91c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110829 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110829 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110829 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110829 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110829 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110829 #edb/ref 1045 "Combining the Rules of Exponents" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110829 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186111531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110829 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110829 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110829 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "812cb5a9-d9bb-5df5-bcad-60b2e4bca49c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1045 "Midpoints" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186111719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110835 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "01608397-be37-5684-a088-261c3d612344" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111427 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111427 #edb/ref 1061 10013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111427 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f54758a4-a9bd-55d8-8fe5-726424159290" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111427 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111523 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111523 #edb/ref 1061 17217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111523 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e1f34a4c-5543-5841-9a26-631425dd8218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111523 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111524 #edb/ref 1061 17218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111524 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "869d7516-5f01-5ae5-8aa5-fa957c225b74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111524 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111525 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111525 #edb/ref 1061 17336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111525 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "99b9d947-8b7b-5e8a-a9e4-9d2b3a2979a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111525 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111531 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111531 #edb/ref 1061 17999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111531 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8150e1a1-0f41-5dc7-a1db-5e474331d638" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111531 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111678 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111678 #edb/ref 1061 476188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111678 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eab36dd4-7684-5fb5-a213-f02f98e0da39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111678 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111679 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111679 #edb/ref 1061 476189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111679 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "310129a9-81a2-54d5-b227-633c2cb1b8d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111679 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111680 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111680 #edb/ref 1061 476192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111680 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "888ce267-7f48-5074-a1ab-42caf954a38c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111680 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111681 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111681 #edb/ref 1061 476193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111681 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f94c8cb-7345-56bc-b884-f0ac3f242910" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111681 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111682 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111682 #edb/ref 1061 476194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111682 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1e04eac3-4a93-5d72-9a90-2b1099628885" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111682 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111683 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111683 #edb/ref 1061 476197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111683 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d109dfd-fdee-5718-a3d2-a9a96f41ffd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111683 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111684 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111684 #edb/ref 1061 476198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111684 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01172b4e-9bb6-512f-9e14-1b2ad76e2a6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111684 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111685 #edb/ref 1061 476199 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111685 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c8fdb7d5-63c2-55ac-a293-d9536babfe47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111685 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111719 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111719 #edb/ref 1061 8710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111719 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fb8d8caf-a880-55f9-8ec4-027667c4d158" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111719 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111720 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111720 #edb/ref 1061 8711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111720 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "071ff184-b63f-5999-8a03-a464cb40e4a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111720 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111721 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111721 #edb/ref 1061 8712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111721 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b8ed3410-17c6-5b4e-801f-204a8559cabe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111721 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111722 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111722 #edb/ref 1061 8713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111722 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "143b8bf4-dc04-56fe-9f49-a538e4b75644" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111722 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111723 #edb/ref 1061 8714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111723 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "45c8bbe7-572c-5f45-8330-89214603ae4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111723 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111814 #edb/ref 1064 "Some linear equations have **infinitely many solutions.** \n\nFor example, consider the following equation: \n\n$$\nx+5 = x + 5\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to solve this equation by subtracting $x$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =x+5 \\\\ x+5-x & =x+5-x \\\\ 5 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, what does $x$ equal? The statement $5=5$ is *always* true, irrespective of the value of $x.$ This tells us that *any* value of $x$ is a solution to the equation!\n\nDon't believe it? If we substitute any value, say $x=3,$ into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =x+5 \\\\ 3+5 & =3+5 \\\\ 8 & =8.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's important to note that substituting *any* value for $x$ into the equation will lead to a true statement. For example, the values \n\n$$\nx=42,\\qquad x=-\\dfrac 1 4, \\qquad x=1.2345\n$$\n\nare *all* solutions to the original equation.\n\nTo summarize:\n\n- Our original equation has *infinitely many solutions.*\n\n- The solution of the equation is \"*any (real) value of* $x$\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111814 #edb/ref 1065 11389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111814 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "edb6ae26-e191-5d7d-9ce8-ad9ee36655f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111814 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111815 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2qx = 10x,\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. If we're given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the value of $q?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{2q}_{\\large a} \\ x = \\underbrace{10}_{\\large b} \\ x\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n$$\na = 2q, \\qquad b = 10.\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b.$ Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2q = 10.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2q & =10 \\\\ \\frac{2q}{2} & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ q & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2qx = 10x$ has infinitely many solutions if $q = 5.$\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by substituting $q = 5$ into our original equation. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2qx & =10x \\\\ 2(5)x & =10x \\\\ 10x & =10x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand simplifying this equation by subtracting $10x$ from both sides yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10x & =10x \\\\ 10x-10x & =10x-10x \\\\ 0 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is true for all values of $x.$\n\n**Note:** The constant $q$ in this problem is sometimes called a **parameter.**" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111815 #edb/ref 1065 11486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111815 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0d28dc5e-68de-53d0-a57c-9072a89eccbb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111815 #edb/ref 1067 "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111817 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(s - 2)x + t = 6x + 9,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the values of $s$ and $t?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax+b = cx+d{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(s-2)}_{\\large a} \\ x + \\underbrace{t}_{\\large b} = \\underbrace{6}_{\\large c} \\ x + \\underbrace{9}_{\\large d}\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax+b = cx+d$ has infinitely many solutions when $a=c$ and $b=d.$\n\nSo, to determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}})x + {\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}x + {\\color{red}{{9}}}\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we **equate the coefficients** of $x,$ and we **equate the constants:**\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{red}{{9}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $s = 8$ and $t=9,$ our original equation has infinitely many solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111817 #edb/ref 1065 13403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111817 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dc5c5f3e-0731-5ab3-8afd-a2cf24228db1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111817 #edb/ref 1067 "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters in General Linear Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111818 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = bx,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nFor example, each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x=2x,\\,\\frac{1}{3}x=\\frac{1}{3}x\\,-2.16x=-2.16x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo show that the general equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b,$ we can substitute $a$ for $b$ in our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =bx \\\\ ax & =(a)x \\\\ ax & =ax\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =ax \\\\ ax-ax & =ax-ax \\\\ 0 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation, $0=0,$ is always true, regardless of the value of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $ax = bx$ has infinitely many solutions whenever $a=b,$ and any value of $x$ is a solution to the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111818 #edb/ref 1065 13429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111818 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8428afc7-2e9a-55d0-9f13-7a2acdf8fd7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111818 #edb/ref 1067 "Conditions for an Equation To Have Infinitely Many Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111819 #edb/ref 1064 "Each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n2x + 1 = 2x + 1, \\qquad \\dfrac13x -2 = \\dfrac13x -2, \\qquad 1-x = 1 - x\n$$\n\nMore generally, suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d,\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c,$ and $d$ are constants. Then, this equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d.\n$$\n\nNote that if we substitute $c=a$ and $d=b$ into this equation, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax+b & =cx+d \\\\ ax+b & =(a)x+(b) \\\\ ax+b & =ax+b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax+b & =ax+b \\\\ ax+b-ax & =ax+b-ax \\\\ b & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation, $b = b,$ is always true. Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions, and any real value of $x$ is a solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111819 #edb/ref 1065 13430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111819 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7498a4f8-36b5-5d1d-b953-893581bfa9aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111819 #edb/ref 1067 "Conditions for a General Linear Equation To Have Infinitely Many Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111835 #edb/ref 1064 "The **midpoint** of a line segment is the point that splits it into two segments of equal length. For example, $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$ shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ede582389ad936b02a6074a3fb6543e730ae955baf9784147f133f9492a35708.png)\n\nThe midpoint splits the line segment into two congruent segments,\n\n$$\n\\overline{PM}\\cong \\overline{QM}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is twice as long as the segments $\\overline{PM}$ and $\\overline{QM}$:\n\n$$\nPQ = 2PM, \\qquad PQ = 2QM\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111835 #edb/ref 1065 2165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111835 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9e476e67-9dd4-5098-be96-fc805e9e97f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111835 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111857 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's find the midpoint $M$ of the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ on a number line, if $A$ corresponds to $x_1= 3$ and $B$ corresponds to $x_2=7,$ as depicted below.\n\n![](images/11/115797e11eb3e434d5faefe412a8cefea216b6190436d408d199ac31d0da7210.png)\n\n \n\nThe midpoint of a line segment splits it into two segments of equal length.\nTo find the number $x_m$ corresponding to $M,$ we use the following **midpoint formula**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{m} & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x_1=3$ and $x_2=7$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{m} & =\\frac{3+7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the midpoint $M$ corresponds to $x_m=5.$\n\n![](images/fc/fc0a47734756e12ec117db92d8f0273ae564c03ead88ce86eac82005be6feaf4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111857 #edb/ref 1065 2347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111857 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7b14d3c0-0c84-55bc-be21-3359fbc5f6ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111857 #edb/ref 1067 "The Midpoint Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111884 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's recap some of the rules for exponents.\n\n- **Product rule**: to multiply powers of the same base, add the exponents.\n\n- **Quotient rule**: to divide powers of the same base, subtract the exponents.\n\n- **Power rule**: to raise a power to another power, multiply the exponents.\n\n- **Power of product rule**: to raise the product of two numbers to a power, raise each number to that power, and then multiply.\n\n- **Power of quotient rule**: to raise a fraction to a power, raise both the numerator and the denominator to that power.\n\nWe can use a combination of these rules to simplify expressions containing exponents. Let's see an example of this." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111884 #edb/ref 1065 3275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111884 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7c442391-b257-5c16-9fe0-292a45e3c172" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111884 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234249 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553481 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234249 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234249 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234249 #edb/ref 1071 "${(\\frac{6}{7})}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{3}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234249 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ce2bfa1c-5e37-5680-930e-c063dd6092a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234249 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite the first factor using the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{6}{7})}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{3}=\\frac{{6}^{8}}{{7}^{8}}\\cdot {7}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the fractional part of our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{6}^{8}}{{7}^{8}}\\cdot {7}^{3}={6}^{8}\\cdot \\frac{{7}^{3}}{{7}^{8}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the quotient rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n{6}^{8}\\cdot \\frac{{7}^{3}}{{7}^{8}}={6}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{3-8}={6}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{-5}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295383 #edb/ref 1080 "7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295383 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295383 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "14a449b2-0875-581b-a2ec-8813a227c1de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295530 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295530 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295530 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2d6f283f-3592-5248-baf7-ca782b7d8c9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295633 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295633 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295633 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4009ef03-e229-5324-9dbd-214305b60cd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295715 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295715 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186295715 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4dfca85a-c698-5e36-ba67-02a55f783f85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296371 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296371 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296371 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d199324f-9733-59f2-82cf-8e4f45ae01ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296621 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296621 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296621 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fe4c39bf-4560-5825-a22a-76a5df230cbf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296679 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186295383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296679 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186295530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296679 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186295633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296679 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186295715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296679 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186296371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296679 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296679 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296679 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186295383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296679 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1739b830-13d4-586f-9761-4372557e83e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296811 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186296621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296811 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296811 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296811 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186296621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186296811 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "58355121-2d5d-5aa8-91c2-79f41befa005" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186476103 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186110719.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476103 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "393aec7c-08a3-58cd-ac4d-3705704af62b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476179 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476187 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476179 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476179 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(6p-6)x=36x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476179 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c6ffd8f-aed0-5462-a678-623c3eb10c91" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476179 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=6p-6,\\,b=36.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n6p-6=36.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6p & =42 \\\\ p & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476183 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476194 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476183 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476183 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(5p-3)x=37x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476183 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8af6ded4-48a3-5261-b74f-acd5512ac6e3" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476183 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=5p-3,\\,b=37.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5p-3=37.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5p & =40 \\\\ p & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476185 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476181 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476185 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476185 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(3p-1)x=17x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476185 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a0e66191-a3c9-5572-8227-0f5cb2926471" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476185 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=3p-1,\\,b=17.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n3p-1=17.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p & =18 \\\\ p & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476189 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476178 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476189 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476189 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(7p-1)x=20x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476189 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c2e51c73-6455-54ee-866f-56127d07e55b" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476189 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=7p-1,\\,b=20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n7p-1=20.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7p & =21 \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476193 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186476195 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476193 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476193 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n7px=2x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476193 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e320282a-d753-5114-aed7-fb576099a4d8" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476193 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=7p,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n7p=2.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476197 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186110720.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186476197 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "c05f43b0-71c3-5af5-bc14-8a813a2da1af" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477025 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477006 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477006 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(m+7)x+8n=9x+88\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ and $n$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m+n?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477006 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "436cab40-c84e-51f6-a518-ef754e1d23e8" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477006 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $m$ and $n$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(m+7)x+8n=9x+88\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+7=9.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n8n=88.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $m=2$ and $n=11,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+n=2+11=13.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477007 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477019 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477007 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477007 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+16)x+2t=7x+12\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s+t?$\n\n$s+t=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477007 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "475d1245-f209-5a1a-94d0-b61255568fb1" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477007 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+16)x+2t=7x+12\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n-s+16=7.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\ns=9.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n2t=12.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $s=9$ and $t=6,$ we have\n\n$$\ns+t=9+6=15.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477016 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477015 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477016 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477016 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+15)x+4t=3x+4\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s+t?$\n\n$s+t=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477016 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "af3151d7-d56f-5c03-a92e-cf11481af995" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477016 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+15)x+4t=3x+4\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n-s+15=3.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\ns=12.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n4t=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $s=12$ and $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\ns+t=12+1=13.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477018 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477004 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477018 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477018 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(m+8)x+8n=19x+48\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ and $n$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m+n?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477018 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bae891b2-2044-5361-b2ef-88211b0b3d9f" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477018 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $m$ and $n$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(m+8)x+8n=19x+48\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+8=19.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n8n=48.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $m=11$ and $n=6,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+n=11+6=17.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186477021 #edb/ref 